! ja_ax1a "I-BOX 1", the %BEW% and %SIC-BEW% blocks, PDF p.58, octal
! 006027.40-006047.00.  Its banners: "%BEW% **** BRANCH ENABLED + WAIT
! **** %BEW%" and "%SIC-BEW% **** STORE IC IF BRANCH ENABLED + WAIT".
!
! WHY THIS BLOCK.  BEW is the halt idiom every program in this suite
! ends with and NOTHING here has ever checked its encoding against a
! listing; the SIC prefix ON a BEW is a combination no transcription
! here covers at all.  Four BEW blocks and one SIC-BEW block, 31 rows.
!
! WHAT BEW DOES, and the plan used to have this backwards.  Reference
! Manual printed p.40: "This instruction provides a means of stopping
! program execution.  The interruption mechanism is enabled, and the
! instruction counter contents are replaced with bits 0-18 of the
! effective address of the operation as in BRANCH ENABLED.  However,
! no further instruction will be executed until after an interruption
! occurs."  So BEW BRANCHES and then waits.  docs/NEXT.md said this
! block drove BEW "from the interval timer and fall through"; it does
! not, and the listing shows why the reading was wrong -- the "BD,$"
! after each BEW sits at the FALL-THROUGH address and is the hang a
! machine that failed to branch would reach.
!
!   ISBE37  BEW,ISBE38   prints 6031.60 00  at 006030.40
!           BD,$         prints 6031.04 00  at 006031.00
!   ISBE38  BD,$+.32     prints 6032.04 00  at 006031.40
!
! 0o60 has bit 18 set, so BEW's target is 006031.40 -- ISBE38, two
! half-words past the hang.
!
! THIS IS AN ENCODING ORACLE AND A WEAK RUN TEST, said plainly.
! Because BEW always branches, control reaches the target whether the
! WAIT is modelled or not, so no arrangement of these rows can witness
! it.  Seeing the wait needs an instruction at the target that must not
! run until an interruption arrives, and the only source of one here is
! the interval timer -- which this model does not tick.  That is why
! every block below opens by loading $IT.  The encodings stand on their
! own; the run does not, and run_tests.sh records it that way.
!
! Transcription standard: bit-exact against the printed machine code,
! verify_ax1a_bew_listing.py.

        SLC, (8)6027.40
        SV,  $X1, $IT              ! -FOR
        BE,  $+.32                 ! -BEW TEST
ISBE37  BEW, ISBE38                ! -BEW TILL I. T. SIGNAL
        BD,  $                     !  the fall-through hang
ISBE38  BD,  $+.32                 ! -SET UP
        LVI, $X1, 1.0              ! -FOR
        SV,  $X1, $IT              ! -NEXT
        BE,  $+.32                 ! -BEW TEST
ISBE39  BEW, ISBE40                ! -BEW TILL I. T. SIGNAL
        BD,  $
        NOP, (8)525252.0           ! -OFFSET IN Y REGS
ISBE40  BD,  $+.32                 ! -SET UP
        LVI, $X1, 1.0              ! -FOR
        SV,  $X1, $IT              ! -NEXT
        BE,  $+.32                 ! -BEW TEST
ISBE41  BEW, ISBE42                ! -BEW TILL I. T. SIGNAL
        BD,  $
ISBE42  BD,  $+.32                 ! -SET UP
        LVI, $X1, 1.0              ! -FOR
        SV,  $X1, $IT              ! -NEXT
        BE,  $+.32                 ! -BEW TEST
ISBE43  BEW, ISBE44                ! -BEW TILL I. T. SIGNAL
        BD,  $
!
! ---- %SIC-BEW%: STORE IC IF BRANCH ENABLED + WAIT ----------------
! SIC is a PREFIX fused with its branch into one 64-bit instruction
! (Reference Manual p.41: "This instruction may always be prefixed to
! one of the half-word branching instructions.  It is executed if and
! only if the associated branching is successful."), which is why the
! two rows below share the word at 006045.00 and both name the same
! target.
ISBE44  BD,  $+.32                 ! -SET UP
        LVI, $X1, 1.0              ! -FOR
        SV,  $X1, $IT              ! -SIC,BEW
        BE,  $+.32                 ! -TEST
ISBE45  SIC, ISBE46+.32
        BEW, ISBE46+.32            ! -SIC + BEW TILL I.T. SIGNAL
ISBE46  BD,  $+1.0
        BD,  $                     ! -BD,$+1.0 + BD, ISBE 46
        LVI, $X1, 1.0              ! -SETS UP
        END, ISBE37
